The [#4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says the ticket is one issue with one fix queued. The reports
merged into it do not say that. Read by tenant, not by subject, they are three problems, and the
queued fix closes only the first one.

<ProblemSplit ticket={4471} {issues} />

<Callout kind="warn">
#4471 is not known. Two of its three problems have no fix queued. Each one now has its own ticket.
</Callout>

## parser

The shared parser reads every date month first. Every tenant on file writes the day first, so a
booked date comes back with day and month swapped: "Our 7 April reminder went out on 4 July". This
is the bug #4471 describes, and the fix that is queued.

## hint

The booking-v3 form prints `MM/DD/YYYY` under its date field. People at day-first tenants type
what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way. The date comes out
swapped too, so the reports look the same, but the parser fix does not touch the form. Only
reports sent through the form of a booking-v3 tenant have this cause.

Intake tags reports `hint` or `parser` from the reporter's browser. The cause depends on the
tenant, so the tag is wrong on many reports in both groups. Do not triage by the tag.

## one-day move

Some dates do not swap. They move back one day: "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto
got Monday. Same meeting." A month-first parse cannot do that. It looks like a date stored as an
instant and shown in the reader's time zone. These are the reports in the desk's own
`oneDayMove` list. The cause table below files them under `parser`, with a footnote. They are a
separate bug.

## Why one ticket hid three

[Decision 0001](/kb/decisions/0001-dedupe-by-subject) merges reports when their subject lines
match. A subject says "wrong date". It does not say which tenant, form or channel the date came
through, and that is where the cause is. Merging on subject put three causes in one ticket and
marked it known. Decision 0001 is accepted, so it stays as written: a new decision record has to
replace it.

